{"id":1498,"date":"2026-08-06T06:45:12","date_gmt":"2026-08-06T06:45:12","guid":{"rendered":"https:\/\/franziskaiseli.com\/?p=1498"},"modified":"2026-08-06T06:45:12","modified_gmt":"2026-08-06T06:45:12","slug":"the-optimists-didnt-die-of-hope","status":"publish","type":"post","link":"https:\/\/franziskaiseli.com\/2026\/08\/06\/the-optimists-didnt-die-of-hope\/","title":{"rendered":"The Optimists Didn\u2019t Die of Hope"},"content":{"rendered":"<p><span style=\"font-weight: 400;\">I\u2019ve always loved Jim Collins\u2019 book <\/span><i><span style=\"font-weight: 400;\">Good to Great<\/span><\/i><span style=\"font-weight: 400;\">. There is a story in it that has stayed with me. Admiral James Stockdale spent more than seven years as a prisoner of war in Vietnam. When asked which prisoners had failed to make it home, Stockdale reportedly replied: \u201cThe optimists.\u201d<\/span><\/p>\n<p><span style=\"font-weight: 400;\">As the story is often told, these were the men who kept saying, \u201cWe\u2019ll be out by Christmas.\u201d Christmas came and went. Then they told themselves, \u201cWe\u2019ll be out by Easter.\u201d Easter came and went too. According to the account, some became overwhelmed by despair.<\/span><\/p>\n<p><span style=\"font-weight: 400;\">It\u2019s a powerful story, although it should be treated as Stockdale\u2019s recollection rather than as proof that optimistic people were less likely to survive. I don\u2019t think optimism was the problem. I think attachment was, not hope itself, but hope attached too tightly to a particular date, outcome or version of the future.<\/span><\/p>\n<p><span style=\"font-weight: 400;\">They didn\u2019t simply believe that life could get better. They attached that belief to a particular date, a particular outcome and a particular version of the future. When reality refused to follow their hopes it began to collapse.\u00a0<\/span><\/p>\n<p><span style=\"font-weight: 400;\">There are different kinds of optimism. Blind optimism says, \u201cEverything will work out exactly the way I want it to.\u201d It ignores reality, tries to control the uncontrollable and clings to a future that exists only in the imagination. Every time life refuses to cooperate, your heart breaks a little.<\/span><\/p>\n<p><span style=\"font-weight: 400;\">Grounded optimism sounds very different. It says, \u201cI don\u2019t know when this will end. I don\u2019t know exactly how this will unfold. I may not get the outcome I hoped for. But I trust that I will find a way to meet what comes.\u201d Grounded optimism does not deny reality. It faces reality squarely while refusing to let reality extinguish hope. It doesn\u2019t demand certainty before choosing courage or insist that life follows a particular timeline. It says, \u201cI cannot control what happens next, but I can choose how I show up.\u201d<\/span><\/p>\n<p><span style=\"font-weight: 400;\">That feels close to the heart of Stoic philosophy. Focus your energy on what is within your control. Release what is not. Meet life as it is, without surrendering your capacity to respond with courage, creativity and humanity. Something many of us are being asked to do right now.<\/span><\/p>\n<p><span style=\"font-weight: 400;\">Whether you\u2019re building a business, raising children, navigating grief, loving someone or pursuing an impossible dream, life rarely unfolds according to your preferred timeline. <\/span><span style=\"font-weight: 400;\"><br \/>\n<\/span><span style=\"font-weight: 400;\"><br \/>\n<\/span><span style=\"font-weight: 400;\">Disappointment, anger, sadness and frustration are unavoidable, but suffering can deepen when your peace depends on controlling something you were never able to control in the first place<\/span><\/p>\n<p><span style=\"font-weight: 400;\">Some people become miserable long term when a relationship fails. Or when their business struggles due to economic forces. Or when the adventure falls through because of cancelled flights. <\/span><span style=\"font-weight: 400;\"><br \/>\n<\/span><span style=\"font-weight: 400;\"><br \/>\n<\/span><span style=\"font-weight: 400;\">When hope is attached to a specific, uncontrollable thing, every setback begins to feel like evidence that hope was foolish. But when hope is attached to who we choose to be, rather than what we need to happen, it becomes much harder to extinguish.<\/span><\/p>\n<p><span style=\"font-weight: 400;\">As an eternal optimist, I have enormous hope for humanity. I have hope for the way we treat one another and for the kind of future we might create together. But I\u2019m also learning that if I attach my happiness to the behaviour of other people, I will spend much of my life disappointed. I cannot control whether other people choose kindness, but I can choose kindness. I cannot control whether the world becomes more courageous, but I can practise courage. I cannot guarantee that everything will turn out the way I imagine, but I can remain curious, loving and awake as it unfolds.<\/span><\/p>\n<p><span style=\"font-weight: 400;\">The strongest form of optimism is believing that even when things don\u2019t work out, we will find a way to show up curiously, courageously and kindly.<\/span><\/p>\n","protected":false},"excerpt":{"rendered":"<p>I\u2019ve always loved Jim Collins\u2019 book Good to Great. There is a story in it that has stayed with me. Admiral James Stockdale spent more than seven years as a prisoner of war in Vietnam. When asked which prisoners had failed to make it home, Stockdale reportedly replied: \u201cThe optimists.\u201d As the story is often [&hellip;]<\/p>\n","protected":false},"author":1,"featured_media":1500,"comment_status":"open","ping_status":"open","sticky":false,"template":"","format":"standard","meta":{"_et_pb_use_builder":"","_et_pb_old_content":"","_et_gb_content_width":"","disable_featured_image":false,"footnotes":""},"categories":[1],"tags":[],"class_list":["post-1498","post","type-post","status